{"parentUuid":"e8d150a7-11ff-4d6c-96cb-b42426bc0db4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01NpqwFRXdKc9dx1HxEW7JFk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Question 5 — `Setup` failure mode\n\nWith Q1 (lazy) + Q4 (single Setup), `Setup` itself does no file IO. Failures only happen at first-write time inside the lazy writer.\n\nWhat can fail:\n- File IO (path can't be created, disk full, permissions) — only at first write.\n- Invalid `Options.Level` — caller's bug, ideally caught at compile.\n- `paths.WorktreeRoot` — only invoked lazily.\n\nThree signatures:\n\n**A. `Setup(ctx, opts) (*slog.Logger, Closer)` — no error return.** Internal first-write failure swaps to stderr and emits a one-shot stderr warning (\"entire: log file unavailable, falling back to stderr\"). main.go is boilerplate-free.\n\n**B. `Setup(ctx, opts) (*slog.Logger, Closer, error)` — error for invalid options only.** File IO still falls back internally. main.go does `if err != nil { fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, err); os.Exit(1) }`. Future-proof for new option-validation.\n\n**C. `Setup` panics on misconfiguration.** Most aggressive. Inappropriate for a CLI binary.\n\n**My recommendation: A.** With `slog.Level` as a typed field, there's nothing to validate up-front. Adding a `, error` we always discard is noise. If we ever add validatable options, we can change the signature then — it's an internal package, no SemVer concern.\n\nThe one-shot stderr warning is important: silent fallback hides \"your log file is broken\" bugs. Concrete: lazy writer wraps the first-open attempt in `sync.Once`; on failure it prints `entire: log file <path> unavailable: <err>; logs going to stderr` exactly once, then routes all subsequent writes to `os.Stderr`.\n\n**A, B, or C?**"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":877,"cache_read_input_tokens":152043,"output_tokens":1895,"ser